BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-new-york-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: New York Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Asian Cultural Council's New York Fellowship puts a cross-d
 isciplinary cohort of artists\, scholars and arts professionals from Asia 
 in the United States for six months to live with a new cultural landscape:
  attending performances\, exhibitions\, workshops and discussions\, and bu
 ilding relationships with peers and with ACC's alumni network. It is delib
 erately an extended pause from daily work with NO obligation to produce or
  present anything. Applicants must be citizens or legal permanent resident
 s of an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region: Afghanistan\, Banglade
 sh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, China (Mainland)\, East Timor\, Hong Kon
 g SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\, Korea\, Laos\, Macau SAR\, Malaysia\, 
 Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Philippines\, Singapore\, Sri Lank
 a\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. and Vietnam. ELIGIBLE DISCIPLINES (16): a
 rchitecture (design\, history and theory)\, archaeology\, art history\, ar
 ts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\, crafts\, curation\, da
 nce\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum studies\, music\, theat
 er and visual art\; video art and photography apply under visual art. Addi
 tional requirements: at least 21 years old by the start of the fellowship\
 , at least five years of professional experience in an eligible discipline
 \, no paid work\, university coursework or teaching (online included) duri
 ng the fellowship\, and enough confident English to handle daily life inde
 pendently\, as the J-1 visa requires. Previous New York Fellows cannot rea
 pply\, and anyone who has held an ACC grant in the last two calendar years
  is ineligible. VISA: fellows must enter the U.S. on an ACC-sponsored J-1 
 visa with no exceptions\, which bars paid work of any kind during the fell
 owship\, including work for a non-U.S. employer and participation in ticke
 ted events even unpaid. WHAT ACC WILL NOT FUND: this is strictly cultural-
 exchange and immersion funding\, not production money. Filming a documenta
 ry\, researching a planned book or film\, mounting an exhibition\, running
  a concert series\, promotional or PR travel\, equipment purchases and ful
 l-time residency programmes are all ineligible\, as is funding used only t
 o travel to a single event. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9
 :59am ET\, applications close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters 
 of recommendation are due 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs Novem
 ber 2026 to April 2027\, notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funde
 d activity must take place between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. App
 ly through the GoApply portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Ap
 plicants may apply to only one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee
 .\n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: New York City a
 nd the wider United States. Open to citizens and legal permanent residents
  of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citize
 ns and U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.\nAward: USD 38\,000 for 
 the six-month fellowship\, including a USD 3\,000 advance for travel costs
 . ACC deducts certain pre-arrival costs from the grant: the USD 220 SEVIS 
 fee\, mandatory travel health insurance (roughly USD 675 to 800) and Inter
 national House housing if you take it (roughly USD 19\,400 to 20\,600 for 
 six months). Fellows can expect USD 10\,000 to 31\,100 in hand after arriv
 al depending on insurance and housing choices. Also included: ACC-organise
 d group activities and institutional visits\, individual programmatic and 
 logistical support\, and entry to the ACC alumni network. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Six months\, in one of two cohorts: 1 January to 30 June 20
 28\, or 1 July to 31 December 2028. ACC assigns the cohort.\n\nMore: https
 ://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York City and the wider United States. Open to citizens and le
 gal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and region
 s in Asia\; U.S. citizens and U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-individual-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: Individual Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Asian Cultural Council's Individual Fellowship funds artist
 s\, scholars and arts professionals from Asia and the U.S. to spend one to
  six months somewhere new or less familiar\, pursuing self-designed resear
 ch and cultural immersion: meeting practitioners\, visiting studios\, muse
 ums\, galleries\, theatres and libraries\, joining workshops and symposia\
 , and doing archival research. ACC is explicit that this is not a producti
 on residency\, not a conference travel grant and not a PR tour. APPLYING J
 OINTLY: two or three people who share research goals can apply together as
  equal partners (one primary applicant\, up to two co-applicants\, no assi
 stant roles)\; the USD 35\,000 cap covers the whole team. Applicants must 
 be citizens or legal permanent residents of an ACC-eligible country\, terr
 itory or region: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, C
 hina (Mainland)\, East Timor\, Hong Kong SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\,
  Korea\, Laos\, Macau SAR\, Malaysia\, Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakist
 an\, Philippines\, Singapore\, Sri Lanka\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. an
 d Vietnam. ELIGIBLE DISCIPLINES (16): architecture (design\, history and t
 heory)\, archaeology\, art history\, arts administration\, arts criticism\
 , conservation\, crafts\, curation\, dance\, ethnomusicology\, film\, lite
 rature\, museum studies\, music\, theater and visual art\; video art and p
 hotography apply under visual art. Additional requirements: at least 21 ye
 ars old by the start\, at least five years of professional experience in a
 n eligible discipline\, no university coursework or teaching during the fe
 llowship\, and confident English if travelling to the U.S. on a J-1 visa. 
 Anyone who has held an ACC grant in the last two calendar years is ineligi
 ble. DESTINATIONS: any eligible country except the one you currently live 
 in\; a place you have lived in for over a year or hold citizenship in need
 s a strong rationale. U.S. fellowships need at least 30 consecutive days a
 nd cannot start before 1 October 2027 because of J-1 processing\; fellowsh
 ips in Asia need 30 cumulative days\, splittable into trips of at least 14
  consecutive days with no more than 14 days between them. ACC sponsors the
  J-1 for U.S. travel\; for Asia you arrange your own visas\, and the fees 
 can go in the budget. WHAT ACC WILL NOT FUND: this is strictly cultural-ex
 change and immersion funding\, not production money. Filming a documentary
 \, researching a planned book or film\, mounting an exhibition\, running a
  concert series\, promotional or PR travel\, equipment purchases and full-
 time residency programmes are all ineligible\, as is funding used only to 
 travel to a single event. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9:5
 9am ET\, applications close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters of
  recommendation are due 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs Novembe
 r 2026 to April 2027\, notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funded 
 activity must take place between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. Apply
  through the GoApply portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Appl
 icants may apply to only one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee.\
 n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: Travel between an
 d within Asia and the United States. Open to citizens and legal permanent 
 residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and regions\, including 
 the U.S. You cannot propose travel to the place where you currently live.\
 nAward: Up to USD 35\,000\, the same cap whether you apply alone or jointl
 y with up to two co-applicants. Covers economy airfare\, ground transport\
 , accommodation\, food and daily living costs\, visa fees\, mandatory trav
 el health insurance\, interpretation and costs tied to the exchange activi
 ties. Also included: ACC logistical and research guidance\, introductions 
 to alumni and professionals\, and visa assistance for U.S. travel. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One to six months\, at least 30 days in the destin
 ation\; ACC prefers two months or more in a single place.\n\nMore: https:/
 /www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Travel between and within Asia and the United States. Open to citi
 zens and legal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories
  and regions\, including the U.S. You cannot propose travel to the place w
 here you currently live.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-graduate-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: Graduate Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:ACC's Graduate Fellowship covers travel and living costs for a 
 small number of graduate students from Asia who come to the U.S. because c
 omparable arts and culture programmes are unavailable or underrepresented 
 at home. The five-year professional experience requirement that applies to
  ACC's other programmes is WAIVED here. Applicants must be citizens or leg
 al permanent residents of an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region: A
 fghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, China (Mainland)\, 
 East Timor\, Hong Kong SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\, Korea\, Laos\, Ma
 cau SAR\, Malaysia\, Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Philippines\,
  Singapore\, Sri Lanka\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. and Vietnam. ELIGIBL
 E DISCIPLINES (16): architecture (design\, history and theory)\, archaeolo
 gy\, art history\, arts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\, c
 rafts\, curation\, dance\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum st
 udies\, music\, theater and visual art\; video art and photography apply u
 nder visual art. Additional requirements: be pursuing a graduate degree in
  one of the 16 eligible disciplines\; be enrolled full-time and in residen
 ce at a U.S. graduate programme\, or hold proof of admission by 1 April 20
 27\; be in the coursework phase\, not the research or thesis phase\; and c
 omplete at least two consecutive semesters of full-time coursework within 
 the grant period. Part-time\, summer-only\, online and non-degree programm
 es are not eligible. ACC does not sponsor student visas\, so the F-1 or eq
 uivalent is between you and your university. Renewal for a second year dep
 ends on ACC's review of your academic record and proof of continued enrolm
 ent. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9:59am ET\, applications
  close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters of recommendation are d
 ue 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs November 2026 to April 2027\
 , notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funded activity must take pl
 ace between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. Apply through the GoApply 
 portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Applicants may apply to o
 nly one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Asia
 n Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: United States\, at the applicant's own
  graduate institution. Open to citizens and legal permanent residents of A
 CC-eligible countries\, territories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citizens an
 d U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.\nAward: USD 15\,000 for one a
 cademic year\, renewable once for a second year at ACC's discretion. Cover
 s travel and living expenses during the coursework phase only\, NOT tuitio
 n and NOT research costs such as fieldwork or equipment. Also included: in
 troductions into ACC's global alumni network. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: One academic year of full-time coursework (Fall 2027 to Spring 2028\, o
 r Spring 2028 to Fall 2028)\, renewable for a second year.\n\nMore: https:
 //www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States\, at the applicant's own graduate institution. Open 
 to citizens and legal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, terr
 itories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citizens and U.S. permanent residents a
 re NOT eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:guggenheim-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Guggenheim Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Guggenheim Fellowship is awarded to mid-career individuals 
 who have already demonstrated exceptional capacity for productive scholars
 hip or exceptional creative ability in the arts\, giving them six to twelv
 e months of funded time to work with as much creative freedom as possible.
  Around 175 to 200 fellows are chosen each year from a field of several th
 ousand applicants (a success rate of roughly five percent)\, across more t
 han 50 fields spanning the creative arts\, the humanities\, the natural sc
 iences and the social sciences\, including architecture\, film\, music\, p
 hotography\, fine arts\, computer science and interdisciplinary work. ELIG
 IBILITY: citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada\
 ; applicants must be mid-career with a significant record of published or 
 exhibited work\, and current students and previous Guggenheim Fellows are 
 not eligible. APPLICATION: through the Foundation's own portal\, which ope
 ns in late summer 2026\, so check the site if it is not yet live. The appl
 ication deadline is 15 September 2026 with no late submissions accepted\; 
 work samples are due 29 September 2026 and reference letters 13 October 20
 26. Results are announced in late April 2027. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Joh
 n Simon Guggenheim Memorial Foundation\nLocation: Anywhere\; the fellowshi
 p funds the fellow's own work rather than residence at a host institution.
  Open to citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada.
 \nAward: Variable cash grant sized to the applicant's proposed budget and 
 needs\; the Foundation does not publish a fixed figure or range\, and rece
 nt awards have typically run into the tens of thousands of US dollars. Rou
 ghly 175 to 200 fellowships are awarded each year across more than 50 fiel
 ds. The Foundation charges no application fee and has no affiliated applic
 ation consultants.\nDuration: Six to twelve months of supported work\n\nMo
 re: https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Anywhere\; the fellowship funds the fellow's own work rather than 
 residence at a host institution. Open to citizens and permanent residents 
 of the United States and Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nebraska-arts-council-artist-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261117
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261118
SUMMARY:Deadline: Nebraska Arts Council: Individual Artist Fellowship 2026 
 (Performing Arts)
DESCRIPTION:The Nebraska Arts Council's Individual Artist Fellowship awards
  up to $5\,000 for a one-year period to support professional development: 
 creating new work\, presentations\, training and/or research. The fellowsh
 ip rotates among three umbrella categories\; the 2026 round accepts Perfor
 ming Arts applications in theater/playwriting\, choreography\, filmmaking/
 screenwriting\, and music/sound. ELIGIBILITY: Nebraska resident for at lea
 st 24 months before application\, remaining resident during the fellowship
  year (attestation required)\; 19 or older\; not enrolled in an undergradu
 ate program in the discipline applied for (graduate and certificate progra
 ms allowed)\; applicants must be the originator of the submitted work\, no
 t an interpreter. Previous winners may reapply after three years with new 
 work if prior grant requirements were completed. SUBMISSION: artist biogra
 phy\, artist statement (name redacted\; themes\, influences\, techniques)\
 , and five work samples with descriptions or synopses. Questions: meagan.d
 ion@nebraska.gov. DEADLINE: 17 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Nebraska Ar
 ts Council\nLocation: Nebraska\, USA. Applicants must have been Nebraska r
 esidents for at least 24 months before applying and remain residents durin
 g the fellowship year.\nAward: Up to $5\,000 for a one-year fellowship sup
 porting professional development\, including the creation of new art\, pre
 sentations\, training and/or research. No application fee.\nDuration: One-
 year fellowship period\n\nMore: https://www.artscouncil.nebraska.gov/apply
 /individual-artist-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.artscouncil.nebraska.gov/apply/individual-artist-fellowship
 /?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Nebraska\, USA. Applicants must have been Nebraska residents for a
 t least 24 months before applying and remain residents during the fellowsh
 ip year.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:global-artivism-governance-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260716
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260717
SUMMARY:Deadline: Global Artivism: Governance Fellow
DESCRIPTION:Global Artivism\, a Global Majority-led movement that engages t
 he power of arts and culture toward a just world on a safe planet\, is con
 tracting a Governance Fellow for a mentored\, time-limited engagement focu
 sed on governance systems\, democratic institutional design\, and Global M
 ajority-led movement infrastructure. The contractor supports the developme
 nt and maintenance of Global Artivism's governance architecture\, includin
 g documentation\, committee coordination\, and research that supports the 
 Democratic Transition process\, and is expected to take initiative on assi
 gned tasks and grow through structured mentorship. The movement is governe
 d by a Global Council with a Movement Secretariat coordination team\; the 
 Fellow coordinates primarily with the Governance Lead alongside the Operat
 ions Director\, Secretariat members\, and Council committees (coordination
  relationships do not constitute supervisory authority). KEY RESPONSIBILIT
 IES: governance documentation (frameworks\, committee charters\, terms of 
 reference\, process documentation\; maintaining an organized\, current\, c
 onsistently formatted document library\; preparing materials for Global Co
 uncil meetings)\; committee coordination (scheduling\, materials\, meeting
  notes\, follow-up\, trackers of decisions and action items\, agendas and 
 briefing packs)\; Democratic Transition and electoral process support (res
 earch on governance models\, electoral processes\, and democratic particip
 ation mechanisms\; drafting materials for the Democratic Transition Roadma
 p 2025-2027\; preparing electoral framework and testing documentation\; tr
 acking milestones)\; and governance research (comparative analyses and bri
 efing notes\; staying current on distributed governance\, participatory de
 mocracy\, and decentralized leadership\; contributing to knowledge product
 s). REQUIRED: manages multiple documentation tasks and meets deadlines wit
 h attention to accuracy and formatting (experience via union organizing\, 
 participatory facilitation\, cooperative governance\, or community-based d
 ecision-making counts alongside academic or formal work history)\; good wr
 itten English\; ability to take initiative and work independently\; genuin
 e interest in governance\, movement building\, or institutional design\; a
 bility to operate as an independent contractor\; and commitment to Global 
 Artivism's values. PREFERRED: background in governance\, law\, political s
 cience\, or international development\; interest in Global Majority leader
 ship and distributed power\; civil society/NGO/movement experience\; famil
 iarity with arts\, culture\, or social justice movements\; multilingual ab
 ility in languages relevant to Global South contexts. This is an independe
 nt contractor engagement managed through Global Artivism's fiscal sponsors
 hip with Res Publica US Inc.\; the contractor sets their own hours and met
 hods\, may serve other clients\, and handles their own taxes and local com
 pliance. TO APPLY: submit an expression of interest via the ABM Intelligen
 ce application page\; submissions are reviewed on a rolling basis by ABM's
  consulting team. Expressions of interest close Thursday 16 July 2026\, 10
 pm MYT (UTC+8)\; late submissions will not be reviewed. Priority is given 
 to candidates from the Global South and Global Majority communities\, and 
 community organizing\, grassroots coordination\, and self-taught practice 
 are valued alongside formal credentials.\n\nOrganisation: Global Artivism 
 (a fiscally sponsored project of Res Publica US Inc.)\nLocation: Remote\, 
 open globally. Priority given to applicants from the Global South.\nAward:
  USD 1\,200 to 1\,800 per month (independent contractor fee)\, paid on rec
 eipt of monthly invoices. No employee benefits\, paid leave\, or insurance
 \; contractor covers their own taxes and compliance.\nDuration: 12-month i
 ndependent contractor engagement from August 2026\, with potential renewal
  subject to funding and mutual agreement. Flexible hours with scheduled ca
 lls across time zones.\n\nMore: https://www.abm-intelligence.com/governanc
 efellow-globalartivism?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://www.abm-intelligence.com/governancefellow-globalartivism?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open globally. Priority given to applicants from the Glob
 al South.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:global-artivism-operations-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260716
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260717
SUMMARY:Deadline: Global Artivism: Operations Fellow
DESCRIPTION:Global Artivism\, a Global Majority-led movement that engages t
 he power of arts and culture toward a just world on a safe planet\, is con
 tracting an Operations Fellow for a mentored\, time-limited engagement foc
 used on organizational infrastructure and systems design. Working alongsid
 e the Operations Director\, the contractor designs operational frameworks 
 spanning financial workflows\, contractor management\, governance alignmen
 t\, and onboarding\, contributing directly to infrastructure decisions whi
 le building practical experience in how a Global Majority-led movement org
 anization functions operationally. The Fellow coordinates primarily with t
 he Operations Director alongside the Governance Lead\, Project Manager\, A
 dmin Coordinator\, and Secretariat members (coordination relationships do 
 not constitute supervisory authority\; decision-making is distributed thro
 ugh governance structures and committees). KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: infrastru
 cture design and framework development (designing and documenting operatio
 nal frameworks\; contributing to staffing models\, organizational structur
 es\, and resource frameworks that enable both centralized and decentralize
 d work\; designing onboarding and operational guidance\; aligning operatio
 nal systems with governance frameworks and democratic decision-making)\; s
 ystems and knowledge documentation (operational guidance and toolkits for 
 distributed teams\; internal knowledge systems such as documentation\, che
 cklists\, and decision trees)\; and capacity building and research (identi
 fying where documentation or systems redesign enables teams to work more i
 ndependently\; flagging operational bottlenecks and proposing solutions\; 
 sharing learning with Council\, committees\, and teams\; researching opera
 tional models relevant to Global Artivism's structure). REQUIRED: manages 
 multiple tasks and meets deadlines with attention to detail and comfort co
 ordinating logistics across a distributed team (experience via coordinatin
 g community events\, grassroots logistics\, or operations in any context c
 ounts alongside formal work history)\; good written English\; comfort with
  digital tools (Google Workspace\, Slack\, or similar)\; genuine interest 
 in operations\, organizational systems\, or movement infrastructure\; abil
 ity to operate as an independent contractor\; and commitment to Global Art
 ivism's values. PREFERRED: background in business administration\, project
  management\, or finance\; interest in international civil society\, movem
 ent building\, or Global Majority leadership\; civil society/NGO/community
  experience\; familiarity with digital project management or operations to
 ols\; multilingual ability in languages relevant to Global South contexts.
  This is an independent contractor engagement managed through Global Artiv
 ism's fiscal sponsorship with Res Publica US Inc.\; the contractor sets th
 eir own hours and methods\, may serve other clients\, and handles their ow
 n taxes and local compliance. TO APPLY: submit an expression of interest v
 ia the ABM Intelligence application page\; submissions are reviewed on a r
 olling basis by ABM's consulting team. Expressions of interest close Thurs
 day 16 July 2026\, 10pm MYT (UTC+8)\; late submissions will not be reviewe
 d. Priority is given to candidates from the Global South and Global Majori
 ty communities\, and community organizing\, grassroots coordination\, and 
 self-taught practice are valued alongside formal credentials.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Global Artivism (a fiscally sponsored project of Res Publica US Inc.)
 \nLocation: Remote\, open globally. Priority given to applicants from the 
 Global South.\nAward: USD 1\,000 to 1\,500 per month (independent contract
 or fee)\, paid on receipt of monthly invoices. No employee benefits\, paid
  leave\, or insurance\; contractor covers their own taxes and compliance.\
 nDuration: 12-month independent contractor engagement from August 2026\, w
 ith potential renewal subject to funding and mutual agreement. Flexible ho
 urs with scheduled calls across time zones.\n\nMore: https://www.abm-intel
 ligence.com/operationsfellow-globalartivism?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.abm-intelligence.com/operationsfellow-globalartivism?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open globally. Priority given to applicants from the Glob
 al South.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iias-asia-in-the-world-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: IIAS: Asia in the World Fellowship Programme 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:The Asia in the World Fellowship Programme\, run by the Interna
 tional Institute for Asian Studies (IIAS) at Leiden University\, is a nine
 -month\, two-phase fellowship pairing a residency at a partner institution
  in Asia\, Africa\, Latin America\, or Europe (Phase 1) with six months in
  residence at IIAS in Leiden\, the Netherlands (Phase 2). The programme su
 pports interdisciplinary\, humanities- and social-science-based research e
 ngaging Asia and its connections with the wider world. SUPPORT during the 
 Leiden phase: EUR 1\,250 per month stipend\, EUR 1\,200 per month housing 
 allowance\, and a travel grant\; the Phase 1 stipend is provided by the co
 -host institution. ELIGIBILITY: scholars and researchers worldwide\; open 
 internationally. APPLICATIONS open 1 July 2026\; deadline 10 September 202
 6. Apply via the IIAS fellowships page. NOTE: dates confirmed via secondar
 y sources at time of entry (the funder page was not directly reachable)\; 
 verify before applying.\n\nOrganisation: International Institute for Asian
  Studies (IIAS)\, Leiden University\nLocation: Two phases: a partner insti
 tution in Asia\, Africa\, Latin America\, or Europe (Phase 1)\, then Leide
 n\, Netherlands (Phase 2). Open internationally.\nAward: Leiden phase: EUR
  1\,250 per month stipend plus EUR 1\,200 per month housing allowance\, pl
 us a travel grant\; the Phase 1 stipend at the co-host institution is set 
 by that institution. No application fee.\nDuration: 9 months (two phases)\
 n\nMore: https://www.iias.asia/fellowships/asia-in-the-world?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iias.asia/fellowships/asia-in-the-world?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Two phases: a partner institution in Asia\, Africa\, Latin America
 \, or Europe (Phase 1)\, then Leiden\, Netherlands (Phase 2). Open interna
 tionally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tba21-academy-tentacular-fellowship-ocean-space-venice-2026-2027@artifi
 cialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
SUMMARY:Deadline: TBA21-Academy: Tentacular Fellowship 2026-2027 (Ocean Spa
 ce\, Venice)
DESCRIPTION:TBA21-Academy\, in collaboration with NICHE Centre for Environm
 ental Humanities and the UNESCO Chair on Water\, Heritage and Sustainable 
 Development at Ca' Foscari University\, is launching the first edition of 
 the Tentacular Fellowship\, an initiative-building fellowship based at Oce
 an Space and at Ca' Foscari in Venice. Conceived as a long-term platform f
 or artistic research and ecological engagement\, Ocean Space works to deve
 lop new forms of action capable of moving beyond awareness-raising to prod
 uce real\, tangible territorial effects through situated collaboration wit
 h the communities\, practitioners\, researchers and civic actors already w
 orking within the Lagoon of Venice. The fellowship invites a practitioner 
 or collective to initiate\, consolidate or expand a situated initiative in
  the Lagoon through the creation of a Tentacular Unit: a collaborative\, i
 nterdisciplinary platform able to gather artists\, students\, researchers\
 , institutions\, local communities and other actors around a specific soci
 o-environmental challenge. Through situated research\, alliance-building a
 nd public-facing formats\, the fellow helps build the social\, institution
 al and ecological conditions for the initiative to take root and grow over
  time. STRUCTURE: the two-year fellowship runs in two phases. In Phase 1 (
 incubation) the fellow is awarded a EUR 3\,000 grant to support independen
 t research and initiative development. In Phase 2 (activation) the fellow 
 is awarded a EUR 8\,000 grant plus a EUR 15\,000 activation budget\, for t
 otal fellowship support of EUR 26\,000. Details on the application process
 \, evaluation criteria and frequently asked questions are in the full open
 -call document. Application deadline: 5 July 2026\, 11:59pm CET. Apply via
  the online form linked from the open-call page.\n\nOrganisation: TBA21-Ac
 ademy\, with NICHE Centre for Environmental Humanities and the UNESCO Chai
 r on Water\, Heritage and Sustainable Development at Ca' Foscari Universit
 y\nLocation: Ocean Space and Ca' Foscari University\, Venice\, Italy\; sit
 uated work in and with the communities of the Lagoon of Venice.\nAward: To
 tal fellowship support EUR 26\,000. Phase 1 (incubation): EUR 3\,000 grant
  for independent research and initiative development. Phase 2 (activation)
 : EUR 8\,000 grant plus a EUR 15\,000 activation budget. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: 15 months\, September 2026 to November 2027\n\nMore: https:/
 /tba21.org/oceanspace-research-tentacular-fellowship?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://tba21.org/oceanspace-research-tentacular-fellowship?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ocean Space and Ca' Foscari University\, Venice\, Italy\; situated
  work in and with the communities of the Lagoon of Venice.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:harvard-radcliffe-institute-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Harvard Radcliffe Institute: Fellowship Program 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:The Harvard Radcliffe Institute Fellowship offers scientists\, 
 writers\, scholars\, public intellectuals\, and artists a year to pursue a
 mbitious projects in a vibrant interdisciplinary setting amid the resource
 s of Harvard University. The Institute welcomes innovative work that confr
 onts pressing social\, scientific\, and policy issues and seeks to engage 
 audiences beyond academia. Reflecting Radcliffe's history\, it welcomes (b
 ut does not limit eligibility to) proposals focused on women\, gender\, an
 d society\, or that draw on the Schlesinger Library's collections. It also
  invites proposals relevant to the Institute's 2024-2029 focus area\, acad
 emic freedom and connecting across difference (intellectual virtues\, free
  and open inquiry\, diversity of thought\, political polarization\, peace 
 and conflict\, inequality\, religious pluralism\, and related higher-educa
 tion policy issues)\, including work that constructively challenges discip
 linary orthodoxies or advances transformative perspectives. SUPPORT: USD 7
 8\,000 stipend plus USD 5\,000 for project expenses\, with relocation\, ho
 using\, and childcare funds available\; fellows must reside in the Cambrid
 ge/Boston area from September through May. ELIGIBILITY: open to individual
 s across career stages\; tenure is not required and applicants need not be
  academics. This is NOT a postdoctoral fellowship\; those currently enroll
 ed in a degree program are ineligible\, as are former Harvard Radcliffe fe
 llows (1999-present). Applicants must meet discipline-specific criteria. A
 PPLY: register on the online portal and select an area (Humanities and Soc
 ial Sciences\; Creative Arts\; Nonfiction and Journalism\; or Science\, En
 gineering\, and Mathematics). Materials: application form\, CV\, 1\,400-wo
 rd project proposal (with bibliography when appropriate)\, a writing or wo
 rk sample\, and three references. DEADLINES: humanities\, social sciences\
 , creative arts\, and nonfiction and journalism by 10 September 2026\, 5pm
  ET\; science\, engineering\, and mathematics by 1 October 2026\, 5pm ET.\
 n\nOrganisation: Harvard Radcliffe Institute\nLocation: Cambridge\, Massac
 husetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and reside in the Cambridge/Bosto
 n area for the full fellowship (September through May).\nAward: USD 78\,00
 0 stipend plus an additional USD 5\,000 for project expenses. Relocation\,
  housing\, and childcare funds may also be provided. No application fee.\n
 Duration: One academic year\, September 2027 through May 2028 (residential
 )\n\nMore: https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/applicat
 ion-information?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/application-info
 rmation?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and res
 ide in the Cambridge/Boston area for the full fellowship (September throug
 h May).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T121747Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Foundation: Fellowship 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:Founded by the artist and philanthropist Jerome Hill\, the Cama
 rgo Foundation has run this residency in Cassis on the Mediterranean coast
  since 1971\, and more than 1\,500 fellows have been through it. Fourteen 
 fellowships are awarded per cycle\, split roughly seven artists and seven 
 scholars or thinkers\, which is the point: the interdisciplinary cohort is
  the programme\, not a side effect. IMPORTANT: the Foundation has stated t
 hat this is the FINAL fellowship awarded through a public open call before
  it moves to a new selection process from 2027\, so if you have been meani
 ng to apply\, this is the cycle. RESEARCH\, NOT PRODUCTION: this is delibe
 rately structured as a research residency. You propose a project or line o
 f inquiry\, which can be development of a new work\, research preceding fu
 ture creative work\, or a broader investigation in your practice. Visual a
 rtists are explicitly reminded that research is prioritised over productio
 n\, and the Foundation supplies no art materials\, technical equipment or 
 artwork shipping\, so anything needing extensive fabrication is a poor fit
 . Fellows lead one resident session built around their own research or met
 hodology\, and attendance at these sessions is mandatory for everyone. Opt
 ional public Open Studios and introductions to organisations across the Ai
 x-Marseille-Provence region are also part of it. ELIGIBILITY: professional
  artists\, scholars and thinkers of any nationality. Artists should have a
 t least five years of professional practice and a mature body of work evid
 enced by exhibitions\, performances\, publications\, awards or grants. Per
 formance artists\, theatre directors\, choreographers\, multidisciplinary 
 artists\, writers\, filmmakers\, composers and visual artists are all elig
 ible within their categories. Collaborative applications of up to three me
 mbers are accepted. APPLICATION: four separate written responses covering 
 the project\, the research questions\, why Camargo specifically\, and why 
 now in your career\; reviewers are reading for a coherent research traject
 ory rather than a production proposal. An applicant webinar runs 1 Septemb
 er 2026. Deadline 1 October 2026 at 17:59 EST / 23:59 CET\; notifications 
 April 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Fondation Camargo\nLocation: Cassis\, France 
 (Mediterranean coast)\nAward: EUR 3\,500 stipend (EUR 350 per week for 10 
 weeks)\, basic transportation to and from Cassis\, a furnished apartment o
 n campus\, and a studio or appropriate workspace\, plus a reference librar
 y\, music and conference room and open-air theatre. Art materials\, techni
 cal equipment and shipping of artworks are NOT provided. No application fe
 e listed.\nDuration: Ten weeks. Fall 2027 session: 8 September to 17 Novem
 ber 2027. Spring 2028 session: 23 February to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https:/
 /camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cassis\, France (Mediterranean coast)
END:VEVENT
